UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 17, Lesson 2: Communication games', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Communication games build listening accuracy: follow multi-step instructions, pass messages carefully and check understanding.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWhy should the speaker check understanding before a team starts a task?\n\nFEEDBACK\nTo catch missed steps and prevent conflict.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBlaming listeners only ignores unclear instructions.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPlay two communication games. Note one speaking tip and one listening tip that improved success.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Communication games build listening accuracy: follow multi-step instructions, pass messages carefully and check understanding.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Practise and apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-RRS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Play two communication games. Note one speaking tip and one listening tip that improved success. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: To catch missed steps and prevent conflict. Reasoning: I used game by attending to listen rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Blaming listeners only ignores unclear instructions. Correction: To catch missed steps and prevent conflict. Why: the correction restores accurate use of game.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Blaming listeners only ignores unclear instructions. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating game on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with game, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"To catch missed steps and prevent conflict. This matches the lesson skill for game.","options":["Copy the example and call that independent work.","To catch missed steps and prevent conflict.","Blaming listeners only ignores unclear instructions.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting."],"question":"Why should the speaker check understanding before a team starts a task?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Blaming listeners only ignores unclear instructions. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Communication games build listening accuracy: follow multi-step instructions, pass messages carefully and check understanding.","To catch missed steps and prevent conflict.","Careful use of listen with clear evidence.","Blaming listeners only ignores unclear instructions."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising game?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named listen, not by copying the model.","options":["Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the listen used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence."],"question":"A learner is practising game. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"To catch missed steps and prevent conflict. uses safe, healthy, respectful choices appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Copy the example and call that independent work.","To catch missed steps and prevent conflict.","Blaming listeners only ignores unclear instructions.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about game?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about game.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Blaming listeners only ignores unclear instructions."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about listen?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Example: telephone with kindness rules; instruction obstacle course with one speaker.","Blaming listeners only ignores unclear instructions."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Communication games build listening accuracy: follow multi-step instructions, pass messages carefully and… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Blaming listeners only ignores unclear instructions.","Play two communication games. Note one speaking tip and one listening tip that improved success.","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example and call that independent work."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of game?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating game on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: To catch missed steps and prevent conflict. Reasoning: I used game by attending to listen rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with game, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Blaming listeners only ignores unclear instructions. Correction: To catch missed steps and prevent conflict. Why: the correction restores accurate use of game.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y4-w17-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise game.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Health, Wellbeing and Relationships","success_criteria":["I can use game with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify listen and explain why it matters.","I can practise instruction independently on a new prompt.","I can notice team needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Communication games build listening accuracy: follow multi-step instructions, pass messages carefully and check understanding. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Communication games build listening accuracy: follow multi-step instructions, pass messages carefully and check understanding.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about game?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: telephone with kindness rules; instruction obstacle course with one speaker.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWhy should the speaker check understanding before a team starts a task?\n\nFEEDBACK\nTo catch missed steps and prevent conflict.","explain":"Communication games build listening accuracy: follow multi-step instructions, pass messages carefully and check understanding.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Blaming listeners only ignores unclear instructions. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Communication games.","caption":"Clear speak + listen.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Instructions</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">check understanding</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Games</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">accurate listen</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: telephone with kindness rules; instruction obstacle course with one speaker. Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: